conv2dk1 / conv2dk1_skip width-32 restriction fix, verified end to end through
mlir-aie's actual ml/bottleneck design (the design results/aie/bottleneck_spatial_sweep_npu.log
found capped at tensor_w=32).
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), 2026-09-07, one sitting.

  kernels/bottleneck_sweep/sweep.py --shapes 32x32,32x36,32x40,32x44,32x48,32x45,32x46
  (mlir-aie ironenv, python 3.13, ~.npu/cache cleared before the run)

WHAT WAS FOUND
==============
The previously-recorded "tensor_w=32 is a hard ceiling" (bottleneck_spatial_sweep_npu.log,
docs/DECISIONS.md) was checked only at w=56/64/128 -- nothing between 33 and 48 was
tried, and the "5 x w*256 B" budget it used for the ceiling was itself an approximation:
one of the five Tile(0,4) buffers (wts_buf_02, the 1x1+skip weights) is sized by channels
(input_channels/8 * output_channels = constant 16384 B), not by width, so it does not
grow with w. Testing intermediate widths on the already-fixed conv2dk3 kernel found the
design DOES compile at w=36/40/44 -- but produces wrong output at every one of them.

Root cause (different bug, different file than conv2dk3's): conv2dk1_i8_vector,
conv2dk1_ui8_vector and conv2dk1_skip_i8_vector -- the 1x1 and 1x1+skip kernels
bottleneck.py runs at full tensor_w -- hardcode `iw_32_rem = 0` (conv2dk1) or never
implement a remainder path at all (conv2dk1_skip, a commented-out stub), each guarded
only by an `assert((input_width/4)%8==0)` that is compiled out in the release aiecc
build. Any input_width not a multiple of 32 silently dropped its remainder columns --
not a numeric bug, dead code that was never exercised because every prior run used
tensor_w=32 exactly.

THE FIX
=======
Rewrote all three vector kernels (conv2dk1_i8_vector, conv2dk1_ui8_vector,
conv2dk1_skip_i8_vector) to walk input_width in blocks of N<=4 4-pixel chunks,
addressed directly from the base input/output/skip/weight pointers (index arithmetic,
not incremental pointer state -- the class of bug conv2dk3's aligned block had).
Accumulators stay within AIE2's 6 hardware registers (upstream used 8 concurrently).
input_width also made a true compile-time constant via -DINPUT_WIDTH (same reason
conv2dk3 needed it -- see docs/DECISIONS.md). conv2dk1_skip_ui8_vector (the twin for
uint8 skip, not exercised by bottleneck.py) was left unfixed and is flagged as a
known-equivalent bug, not touched.

VERIFICATION -- kernels/bottleneck_sweep/sweep.py, torch-golden correctness gate
==================================================================================
      HxW     MFLOP   wall ms     hw ms   host ms   hw GOPS  e2e GOPS  ok
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    32x32    142.61    2.1973    1.4892    0.7081      95.8      64.9  yes
    32x36    160.43    2.3154    1.6921    0.6233      94.8      69.3  yes
    32x40    178.26    2.3788    1.8151    0.5637      98.2      74.9  yes
    32x44    196.08    2.7220    1.9796    0.7424      99.1      72.0  yes
    32x45    200.54    2.5244    1.9646    0.5598     102.1      79.4  NO (compiled;
                                                    45 is not a multiple of 4 -- VMAC's
                                                    fundamental granularity, an inherent
                                                    hardware limit, not a fix bug)
    32x46      --     RuntimeError: [aiecc] Compilation failed (L1 ceiling)
    32x48      --     RuntimeError: [aiecc] Compilation failed (L1 ceiling)

FIT (32,36,40,44 only): intercept 229.6 us, marginal GOPS 111.8, r^2 0.99136.

WHAT THIS CHANGES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
=======================================
- The real L1 ceiling for this channel config (256/64/64/256) is tensor_w=44, not 32.
  36/40/44 now verify bit-correct against the torch golden through the full chained
  design (conv1 -> conv3x3 split-worker -> conv1+skip), where the same shapes failed
  correctness before this fix. 45 and above (46, 48) either violate the 4-pixel VMAC
  granularity or exceed Tile(0,4)'s 64 KB local memory -- both real hardware limits,
  not fixable by more kernel engineering.
- Does NOT reopen the CLOSED verdict in bottleneck_spatial_sweep_npu.log: hw GOPS at
  w=44 (99.1) is still far below the CPU's measured 819-1094 GOPS int8 rate. The op
  class still loses on this chip; what changed is the addressable width range, not the
  throughput gap.
- Does NOT unlock ResNet50's real 56x56 conv2_x shape -- 56 is still past the 44-wide
  ceiling. Reaching 56 would need restructuring Tile(0,4)'s buffering (e.g. dropping
  double-buffering on one of the five buffers, or moving the skip-add off that tile),
  not just this kernel fix.

Local mlir-aie checkout only (wheel + clone copies, both patched and kept in sync);
never upstreamed, per project convention (see conv2dk3's own fix).
